{
  "id": "Compound::DB00786 - Gene::4316",
  "verbalization": "Marimastat, a small molecule inhibitor, is shown to bind to multiple MMPs (Matrix Metalloproteinases) through distinct interactions with Mmp23b, Mmp26, and Mmp27. These MMPs are part of a broader pathway involving the matrix metalloproteinase family, which is implicated in various pathological processes, including inflammation and tissue remodeling. The shared involvement of these MMPs in the same pathway suggests a potential mechanism by which Marimastat may modulate the activity of Mmp7, a key member of this family. The consistent binding of Marimastat to different MMPs, coupled with their shared participation in the same biological process, supports the hypothesis that Marimastat may exert its effects through a common mechanism involving the inhibition of MMPs, potentially including Mmp7. The evidence from the visible paths indicates a plausible, mechanistically grounded relationship between Marimastat and Mmp7, supported by the presence of a shared pathway and the binding of Marimastat to multiple MMPs.",
